Here is the commit message:
ice: Shut down VSI with "link-down-on-close" enabled

Disabling netdev with ethtool private flag "link-down-on-close" enabled
can cause NULL pointer dereference bug. Shut down VSI regardless of
"link-down-on-close" state.

Fixes: 8ac7132704f3 ("ice: Fix interface being down after reset with link-down-on-close flag on")
Reviewed-by: Przemek Kitszel <przemyslaw.kitszel@intel.com>
Signed-off-by: Ngai-Mint Kwan <ngai-mint.kwan@intel.com>
Signed-off-by: Wojciech Drewek <wojciech.drewek@intel.com>
Tested-by: Pucha Himasekhar Reddy <himasekharx.reddy.pucha@intel.com> (A Contingent worker at Intel)
Signed-off-by: Tony Nguyen <anthony.l.nguyen@intel.com>

Here is the root cause:
The bug is caused by the improper handling of the "link-down-on-close" flag when disabling the network device, which can lead to a NULL pointer dereference if the VSI is not closed properly before the device is disabled. This situation arises when the device is expected to shut down but fails to do so due to the flag's state, resulting in potential access to uninitialized or invalid memory.

Here is the patch:
  */
 int ice_stop(struct net_device *netdev)
 {
 	struct ice_netdev_priv *np = netdev_priv(netdev);
 	struct ice_vsi *vsi = np->vsi;
 	struct ice_pf *pf = vsi->back;

 	if (ice_is_reset_in_progress(pf->state)) {
 		netdev_err(netdev, "can't stop net device while reset is in progress");
 		return -EBUSY;
 	}

 	if (test_bit(ICE_FLAG_LINK_DOWN_ON_CLOSE_ENA, vsi->back->flags)) {
 		int link_err = ice_force_phys_link_state(vsi, false);

 		if (link_err) {
 			if (link_err == -ENOMEDIUM)
 				netdev_info(vsi->netdev, "Skipping link reconfig - no media attached, VSI %d\n",
 					    vsi->vsi_num);
 			else
 				netdev_err(vsi->netdev, "Failed to set physical link down, VSI %d error %d\n",
 					   vsi->vsi_num, link_err);
+
+			ice_vsi_close(vsi);
 			return -EIO;
 		}
 	}

 	ice_vsi_close(vsi);

 	return 0;
 }



